nat44-ed: keep one outside port per inside endpoint on every worker

ED first tries the inside port as the outside port, so one inside
address:port keeps one outside port for every destination
(endpoint-independent mapping, as Linux masquerading does). With workers
the port space is split per worker (port_per_thread); an inside port
outside the range of the worker that owns the inside address got a random
port for each new destination instead, which made about half of all
sockets behave as symmetric NAT with two workers.

Fold such a port into the worker's range (port % port_per_thread) instead,
so the same inside port always asks for the same outside port. A taken
port still falls back to random attempts. Verified on 2 workers: inside
ports 700, 20000 and 50000 keep one outside port to four peers on both
workers (before: random per peer when out of range).

--- a/src/plugins/nat/nat44-ed/nat44_ed_in2out.c
+++ b/src/plugins/nat/nat44-ed/nat44_ed_in2out.c
@@ -106,9 +106,12 @@
   if (port < port_thread_offset ||
       port >= port_thread_offset + port_per_thread)
     {
-      /* need to pick a different port, suggested port doesn't fit in
-       * this thread's port range */
-      port_offset = snat_random_port (0, port_per_thread - 1);
+      /* The suggested (inside) port is outside this thread's range. Fold
+       * it into the range instead of picking a random port, so one inside
+       * endpoint keeps one outside port for every destination
+       * (endpoint-independent mapping) whichever thread owns its address.
+       * A collision still falls back to random ports below. */
+      port_offset = port % port_per_thread;
       port = port_thread_offset + port_offset;
     }
   u16 attempts = ED_PORT_ALLOC_ATTEMPTS;
